# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PrinceNsamba AI - Git ignore policy
# Nothing generated, nothing secret; everything the project needs to be built,
# tested and run stays versioned. Audited rule by rule: `git ls-files -i -c
# --exclude-standard` is empty (no tracked file collides with a rule) and no
# source, migration, script, test, doc or config file is matched.
# Reproduce the audit with the commands in docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, section 10.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Secrets / environment (NEVER commit) ---------------------------------
# Templates are tracked: only files holding values are excluded.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
# `*.secret` alone did not cover the files the application itself generates:
# resolveSecret() persists `<DATA_DIR>/.secret-<NOM>`, so a DATA_DIR pointing
# anywhere other than ./data left those secrets committable. Both patterns are
# kept so the naming is covered wherever the file is written.
*.secret
**/.secret-*
secrets/
# Temporary admin password written by `npm run admin:bootstrap`.
**/bootstrap-admin-password
**/.session-secret

# --- Runtime data (database, uploads, generated outbox) -------------------
# Everything under data/ is produced by a running instance: the SQLite base
# and its journals, uploaded media, the temporary admin password, generated
# .secret-* files. Nothing there is source, so the whole directory is excluded.
# `data/` is deliberately one exact line (verified with this repository's Git 2.39):
#  - `scripts/lint.js` requires the lines .env, data/, *.db, node_modules/ here,
#    so `npm run lint`, `npm run check` and CI stay green only if it is present;
#  - pruning the whole directory is also the strongest form: no pattern can
#    re-include anything under it, so a loosened rule cannot expose the base.
# A file that genuinely has to live in data/ (none exists today) is then added
# explicitly and only after a secret review:
#     git check-ignore -v data/<chemin>   # which rule blocks it
#     git add -f data/<chemin>            # after verifying nothing secret
# The looser `data/**` form plus `!data/seeds/` and `!data/seeds/**` would also
# track such a file, but no longer satisfies the lint line above.
data/
# Independent safety nets: if `data/` ever disappeared, these still keep the
# runtime artefacts out of the index.
data/**
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
uploads/
tmp/

# --- Build output (regenerated, never hand-edited) ------------------------
# `npm run build` recreates dist/ from tracked sources (Vite, build.outDir =
# dist) and the Dockerfile runs that build inside the image, so the served SPA
# needs no committed artefact.
dist/
dist-client/
coverage/

# --- Node / build ---
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-store/
*.tsbuildinfo
.cache/
.vite/

# --- Editor / OS junk ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
# Only .vscode/settings.json is personal; a shared extensions recommendation
# stays versionnable.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*.swo

# --- Local runtime noise ---
logs/
*.log

# --- Local compose overrides (deployment specific, not shared) ------------
docker-compose.override.yml
docker-compose.override.yaml
